      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess concordance in the measurement of peak expiratory flow (PEF) and forced expiratory volume ino ne second (FEV(1)) between the portable device Piko-1 (Ferraris) and a pneumotachograph."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Forced spirometry (Master Screen Jaeger) was performed according to ATS/ERS norms, selecting the best value of three curves, and three measurements with the Piko-1 were recorded the recommendations of the manufacturer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Eighty patients between 5-18 years of age were studied. Based on the Bland-Altman method, the mean differences obtained were 9.82 (95%Cl: 2.43-17.21) for PEF and 0.17 (95%CL: 0.12-0.21 for FEV(1). The intraclass correlation coefficient was 0.96 (p <0,001; 95%Cl: 0.93-0.97) for PEV(1) and 0.93 (p<0,0001; 95%Cl: 0.89-0.95) for PEF."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Piko-1 offers FEV(1) measurements close to those obtained with forced spirometry, thus allowing more exact patient assessment in home-based follow-up emergency services, or hospital wards."
        }
      ]},
